AWPTCM-T5437 (1.0) — DoS: IP OPTION
tb470 IE520 stack (DUT, awplus_main-20260923-20) · armed on member 3 port3.0.9 · 2026-09-28

RESULT: FAIL — CONFIRMED IE520 product defect. `dos ipoptions` is accepted and reports Enabled, but
        NEVER detects: 0 attacks counted and the port never err-disabled, on BOTH the bridged and
        the routed path, with well-formed IP options and a valid unicast source MAC, at ~3200 pps
        (documented threshold is 20 pps). The other five DoS types DO fire on this same bench
        (see their logs), so the DoS subsystem and the injection method both work here — ipoptions
        specifically is a no-op on the IE520.

  This SUPERSEDES the 2026-09-03 "CONFIG VERIFIED, needs L3 routed path" result and the
  2026-09-04 paused investigation. Both were wrong about the cause:
    - 2026-09-04 found the crafted frames used an ILLEGAL multicast source MAC
      (01:00:01:00:00:01) the switch drops before any counting — a real harness bug, now fixed.
    - But with that fixed (valid unicast src MAC 00:f0:4d:00:77:18), and on a genuinely ROUTED
      path, it STILL does not detect. So "needs L3" was also wrong. It simply does not work.

METHOD (all stimulus = benign ICMP echo carrying an IP option; this is DEFENCE verification)
  Source   : tb470 eth3 (00:f0:4d:00:77:18), cabled to stack member 3 port3.0.9 (bench-state.md).
  Options  : Record-Route (RR, IP ihl=6) and Loose Source Route (LSRR, ihl=7). Both wire-verified
             on eth3 with tcpdump 'ip[0]&0x0f>5' — e.g. "options (RR,EOL) 10.38.215.65 > 10.38.215.2".
  Rate     : 6000 frames in ~1.9 s ≈ 3200 pps, per option, per path — >150x the 20 pps threshold.
  Arm      : `dos ipoptions action shutdown` on port3.0.9 → `show dos interface` = ipoptions Enabled.
             (Arming SUCCEEDS on a master-member port. The 2026-09-04 "Cannot update hardware
             filter" was specific to member 1 / port1.0.1, not a general failure.)

  Path 1 — BRIDGED (port3.0.9 access vlan1; dst MAC 02:1a:eb:91:cc:a1 = x230 as learned on sa2,
           a host BEHIND the switch, not the switch's own router MAC):
             armed + RR    → Attacks detected : 0 ; port connected
             armed + LSRR  → Attacks detected : 0 ; port connected
  Path 2 — ROUTED (scratch vlan90 SVI 10.90.0.1/24; port3.0.9 → vlan90; dst MAC 00:00:cd:37:0d:6f
           = stack router MAC; dst IP 10.38.215.2 in vlan1, so the DUT L3-routes and parses the
           options — `ip routing` on, 10.90.0.0/24 directly connected, confirmed):
             armed + RR    → Attacks detected : 0 ; port connected
             armed + LSRR  → Attacks detected : 0 ; port connected

  Negative control (bridged, disarmed with `no dos ipoptions`, re-fire RR): port stays connected —
  as expected, and it also stays connected while ARMED, which is the defect.

WHY THIS IS A SOLID NEGATIVE
  - Options are real and on the wire (tcpdump), not malformed (ihl 6/7).
  - Source MAC is a valid unicast (the 2026-09-04 illegal-MAC bug is fixed).
  - Both the bridged and the routed path were tested — the old "needs L3" escape is closed.
  - Rate is far above threshold.
  - Same bench, same injection method: teardrop/land/ping-of-death/smurf/synflood all err-disable
    (2026-09-03 logs), so the DoS engine and the transit method work on the IE520.

CROSS-PLATFORM CONTROL (2026-09-28, done — caveat resolved)
  Terrence recabled eth3 to test reference platforms. Same stimulus, same method:
  - AR4050S port1.0.1: the `dos` switchport feature is ABSENT on this build
    (arc-awplus_main-20260924-26) — `dos ?` / `show dos` both "Unrecognized command". Cannot arm;
    not a usable control. (The wiki lists AR4050 for `dos`, but is self-inconsistent and the live
    build agrees with the exclusion.)
  - x230-10GP port1.0.1 (S/N G26ZE80EN, a wiki-listed platform that HAS the feature): armed
    `dos ipoptions action shutdown`, fired the SAME Record-Route frames from eth3 (valid src MAC)
    -> Attacks detected : 1, port ERR-DISABLE. IMMEDIATE detection.
  ==> The identical stimulus that the IE520 ignored (0 detections, both paths) trips the x230 at
      once. This CONFIRMS the IE520 result is a genuine IE520-specific defect, not a stimulus or
      method artifact. The defect is safe to file against the IE520.

STATE AFTER: port3.0.9 restored to default access vlan1, no dos config, connected. Scratch vlan90
  + SVI removed. tb470 eth3 restored to 10.38.215.65/27. `bench_probe.py run` = MATCH after the run.
  Evidence transcripts were in tb470:/tmp/ckdos (tmpfs, removed at teardown).
